# Mailroom deploy — environment overrides for `docker compose`.
#
# Docker Compose reads `deploy/.env` automatically. Copy this file to `.env`
# (which is gitignored) and fill it in:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     $EDITOR .env
#
# `.env` never enters git. Note the honest trade-off for this test instance: the
# values below land in the container's environment, so they are visible to
# anyone who can run `docker inspect` on the host. The DB passwords, the mail
# master key, the PGP root, the OIDC client secret and the session key are all
# already handled as Docker *secrets* (mounted files under deploy/secrets/,
# never environment variables) for exactly that reason — see the `secrets:`
# block in compose.yml and deploy/README.md §2. What remains in THIS file is
# everything that is not a secret: the issuer, client id, redirect URL, and
# every other tuning knob below.

# --- Where it listens -------------------------------------------------------
MAILROOM_HTTP_PORT=8090

# Which host interface the port is published on. Defaults to 127.0.0.1;
# earlier versions bound the port to 0.0.0.0, so once a TLS proxy was put in front
# the app stayed directly reachable beside it and the proxy was bypassable.
#
# ⚠ IF YOU REACH THIS INSTANCE FROM ANOTHER MACHINE, you need 0.0.0.0 here (or
# the specific interface). A reverse proxy on the same host does not — it can
# reach 127.0.0.1, which is the point.
# MAILROOM_HTTP_BIND=0.0.0.0
MAILROOM_LOG_LEVEL=info
MAILROOM_LOG_FORMAT=json

# ============================================================================
# AUTHENTICATION — pick EXACTLY ONE of the two blocks below.
# The server refuses to start with neither, and refuses to start with a
# PARTIALLY-filled OIDC block (a typo can never silently fall through to
# dev-auth). See deploy/README.md §4.
# ============================================================================

# --- Option A: OIDC (the real path) -----------------------------------------
# Register this app with your identity provider first. Mailroom serves its
# callback at the PATH of MAILROOM_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL (the conventional
# `/auth/callback`; any path works except /auth/login and /auth/logout).
#
# The REDIRECT URL's SCHEME is load-bearing, not cosmetic:
#   * https://…  -> the session cookie is marked Secure, so the browser only
#                   returns it over HTTPS. Use this only if TLS is actually
#                   terminated in front of the container (a reverse proxy). If
#                   the instance is really reached over plain http, the
#                   LOGIN-STATE cookie set at /auth/start is Secure too, so
#                   the browser never sends it back to the callback; the
#                   sign-in page refuses with ?error=expired
#                   (internal/auth/callback.go, page.go's ErrorExpired) rather
#                   than looping silently.
#                   Most providers also require https for a non-localhost host.
#   * http://…   -> cookie not Secure; works over plain http on a private box,
#                   but your provider may refuse to register an http redirect.
#
# MAILROOM_OIDC_ISSUER=https://id.example.com/realms/main
# MAILROOM_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=mailroom
# MAILROOM_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL=https://mail.example.com/auth/callback
#
# ⚠ THE CLIENT SECRET AND THE SESSION KEY ARE NOT IN THIS FILE. They are the
# only two values in the auth block that are actually secret, and this file
# becomes the container's ENVIRONMENT — readable by `docker inspect`, by
# /proc/<pid>/environ, and by anything that lands inside the container. The
# session key alone is enough to mint a valid session for any user, with no
# password and no database access.
#
# They live in deploy/secrets/ instead, and the container reads them as files:
#
#   deploy/secrets/mailroom_oidc_client_secret.txt   <- from your provider
#   deploy/secrets/mailroom_session_key.txt          <- openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Both files must EXIST before the first `docker compose up`, even when you are
# not using OIDC — a missing secret file stops the whole stack. Leave them empty
# in that case. See deploy/README.md §2 for the commands.
#
# OPTIONAL: an operator identity provisioned at BOOT so the declared accounts
# file (secrets/accounts.json) has an owner before anyone logs in. This is NOT
# a login allowlist — under OIDC, ANY identity your provider lets reach this
# app is auto-provisioned on login regardless of what is set here (the access
# gate is your provider's application binding, plus the mailroom-admin group
# claim for the admin role). Leave both unset for a pure multi-user instance
# with no declared accounts.
#
# Getting the subject wrong is worse than leaving it unset — see
# deploy/README.md §4 ("If you DO set MAILROOM_OPERATOR_SUBJECT...") for what
# actually happens and how to read the right value off a trial login instead
# of guessing.
# MAILROOM_OPERATOR_SUBJECT=
# MAILROOM_OPERATOR_EMAIL=you@example.com

# --- Optional: connect Gmail / Outlook by signing in (OAuth) ----------------
# Lets users connect a Gmail or Outlook.com / Microsoft 365 mailbox by signing
# in at the provider instead of typing an app password. Needs YOUR OWN OAuth
# client at each provider; see deploy/README.md ("Signing in to Gmail and
# Outlook"). A provider is offered only when both its id and secret are set;
# setting one without the other refuses to boot. All empty = feature off.
#
# Register exactly this redirect URI at each provider (the server also logs
# the exact values at boot):
#   https://mail.example.com/oauth/callback/google
#   https://mail.example.com/oauth/callback/microsoft
#
# The secrets go in FILES mounted into the container (a compose.override.yml
# `secrets:` entry), never in this file, for the reason given above.
# MAILROOM_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=1234567890-abc.apps.googleusercontent.com
# MAILROOM_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/mailroom_oauth_google_secret
# MAILROOM_OAUTH_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# MAILROOM_OAUTH_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/mailroom_oauth_microsoft_secret
# Default "common" (personal accounts and any organization). "consumers",
# "organizations", a tenant id or a verified domain narrow it.
# MAILROOM_OAUTH_MICROSOFT_TENANT=common
# The origin browsers reach this server at. Default: the origin of
# MAILROOM_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL. Required for OAuth under dev-auth.
# MAILROOM_PUBLIC_URL=https://mail.example.com

# --- Option B: dev-auth (INSECURE escape hatch) -----------------------------
# NO authentication at all: every request that reaches the port is the operator.
# Honored ONLY when the entire OIDC block above is empty. Use it to click around
# an EMPTY instance before OIDC is wired. Never with real mail, never on a
# network you do not fully trust.
#
# ⚠ ALL THREE LINES BELOW MUST BE SET for dev-auth to be honored, and the
# acknowledgement must be the exact phrase. Previously the first two shipped
# UNCOMMENTED, directly beneath a sentence claiming they were commented — so
# copying this file and running `docker compose up -d` produced a reachable
# mailbox with NO LOGIN. They are commented now, and the server additionally
# refuses to start on the subject alone rather than silently obliging.
# MAILROOM_DEV_AUTH_SUBJECT=operator
# MAILROOM_DEV_AUTH_EMAIL=operator@example.com
# MAILROOM_DEV_AUTH_I_UNDERSTAND=this-instance-has-no-authentication

# --- Sync + reaper ----------------------------------------------------------
MAILROOM_SYNC_INTERVAL=5m
# How quickly mail you delete or move in another IMAP client disappears here.
# "0" disables the reaper (and then that mail never leaves its Mailroom folder).
MAILROOM_REAP_INTERVAL=1h

# How many UID SEARCHes Mailroom may send per minute FOR ONE ACCOUNT.
#
# Each mailbox that comes up for reconciliation costs one UID SEARCH, so an
# account with hundreds of folders can ask for hundreds in a single pass. Many
# providers cap this: Migadu answers at most 60 searches per rolling minute and
# refuses the rest with "search rate limit exceeded ... please wait 42s". The
# cap is charged per ACCOUNT, not per connection, so it is shared with sending
# your local changes back to the server AND with your phone and webmail — which
# is why the default is half the observed ceiling rather than all of it.
#
# Raise it only if you know your provider's limit is higher. "0" disables pacing
# entirely, which is right for a server with no such limit (a self-hosted
# Dovecot) and wrong for a hosted mailbox with many folders.
MAILROOM_IMAP_SEARCH_RATE=30

# Gmail accounts (connected with Google sign-in) download at most this many MB
# of mail in any 24 hours. Gmail suspends an account's IMAP access everywhere (the
# person's phone included) when it downloads more than its own daily limit,
# 2,500 MB, so the default stays below it. A large mailbox imports newest
# first, a day's budget at a time. Must be positive.
#MAILROOM_GMAIL_DAILY_DOWNLOAD_MB=2000
# How often Gmail label changes are re-read for imported mail ("0" disables;
# right only once Gmail is known to report label changes another way).
#MAILROOM_GMAIL_LABEL_SWEEP_INTERVAL=6h

# Read/unread and star changes made in another client, for mail servers that
# do not report them (no CONDSTORE: Outlook/Microsoft 365 and some others).
# Every interval the newest WINDOW UIDs of each folder are re-read (flags
# only), and every FULL_INTERVAL the whole folder is, in steps. "0" disables
# the sweep (INTERVAL) or only the whole-folder pass (FULL_INTERVAL). Servers
# with CONDSTORE never run it.
#MAILROOM_FLAG_SWEEP_INTERVAL=5m
#MAILROOM_FLAG_SWEEP_WINDOW=500
#MAILROOM_FLAG_SWEEP_FULL_INTERVAL=6h

# Advanced sync tuning. The defaults are safe and most
# deployments never need to touch these; uncomment only to diagnose or work
# around a specific server.
#   MAILROOM_SYNC_MAX_BACKOFF        default 30m  — cap on a failing account's
#                                     retry backoff.
#   MAILROOM_IMAP_IDLE                default on  — push new mail within
#                                     seconds instead of waiting for the poll;
#                                     a server without IDLE support falls back
#                                     to polling regardless. 0/false/no/off.
#   MAILROOM_IMAP_IDLE_TIMEOUT       default 29m  — how often IDLE is
#                                     re-issued (RFC 2177).
#   MAILROOM_IMAP_POOL                default on  — reuse one warm,
#                                     NOOP-probed connection per account
#                                     instead of dialing fresh every pass.
#                                     0/false/no/off.
#   MAILROOM_IMAP_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT  default 5m   — evict a pooled connection
#                                     unused this long.
# MAILROOM_SYNC_MAX_BACKOFF=30m
# MAILROOM_IMAP_IDLE=
# MAILROOM_IMAP_IDLE_TIMEOUT=29m
# MAILROOM_IMAP_POOL=
# MAILROOM_IMAP_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT=5m

# --- Remote images (ADR-026) -------------------------------------------------
# Proxies remote images through the server instead of letting the reader's
# browser fetch them directly. Default ON — it MOVES an existing fetch, not
# an additional one (§3c constrains it to what the client would have fetched
# anyway). 0/false/no/off to restore the earlier behaviour.
# MAILROOM_IMAGE_PROXY=
#
# With the proxy on, images in mail from senders you allow are fetched when the
# mail ARRIVES rather than when you first open it (ADR-026 amendment). Some senders sign their image links and the links stop working
# after a while; fetching on arrival is what keeps those pictures. A fetched
# image is kept for as long as a message showing it is kept (moving a message
# to Trash keeps it; deleting it for good releases it). The trade: the sender
# learns when the mail was delivered (their server knows that anyway) instead
# of when you opened it. Tracking pixels are never fetched. Default ON;
# 0/false/no/off fetches at open only, as before.
# MAILROOM_IMAGE_PROXY_PREFETCH=
# Mail received longer ago than this is never prefetched, so a first sync or an
# import of years of mail does not contact every sender in it. A positive Go
# duration; default 168h (7 days).
# MAILROOM_IMAGE_PROXY_PREFETCH_MAX_AGE=168h
# Per-user ceiling, in bytes, on images kept because a message shows them. When
# it is reached, new mail's images are no longer fetched on arrival (they load
# when opened, as before) and a warning is logged at most hourly; nothing
# already kept is ever deleted by it. Default 16 GiB (17179869184); 0 or empty
# = the default; a negative value means no limit.
# MAILROOM_IMAGE_PROXY_PREFETCH_MAX_BYTES=17179869184
#
# The prefetch fetches only for mail that passes the same authentication check
# learned placement uses (set MAILROOM_AUTHSERV_IDS to require a DMARC pass;
# without it, mail that did not clearly fail), never for mail in Junk, and only
# from the standard ports (443, 80).

# --- Search -------------------------------------------------------------------
# How often the indexer drains pending changes when idle.
# MAILROOM_INDEX_INTERVAL=5s
# Turns the indexer and search API off entirely, for an operator who wants no
# plaintext index on disk at all. Email/query then rejects text filters
# rather than pretending to search. Default off (search enabled).
# MAILROOM_SEARCH_DISABLED=

# ⚠ --- Trash, purge & change-log retention (ADR-009) ---------
# DESTRUCTIVE DEFAULTS. Read before changing.
#
#   MAILROOM_TRASH_RETENTION    default 720h (30 days). How long a trashed
#                                message may still be restored. The purge
#                                sweep HARD-DELETES it, and reclaims its blob,
#                                once this window closes — there is no
#                                recovery afterwards. Must be positive.
#   MAILROOM_PURGE_INTERVAL     default 1h. How often that sweep runs. Each
#                                run processes up to 500 due messages per
#                                user (internal/purge); a larger backlog
#                                drains across successive hourly sweeps.
#                                Must be positive — there is no "off": Trash
#                                that is never purged grows without bound.
#   MAILROOM_CHANGE_LOG_RETENTION  default 2160h (90 days), floor 168h
#                                (7 days — a shorter value is refused
#                                outright, not just discouraged). How far
#                                back change history is kept before the
#                                weekly compaction job deletes it. A client
#                                that has not synced in longer than this gets
#                                one forced full resync rather than an
#                                incremental one — correct and bounded, but
#                                real client-visible work, not silent.
# MAILROOM_TRASH_RETENTION=720h
# MAILROOM_PURGE_INTERVAL=1h
# MAILROOM_CHANGE_LOG_RETENTION=2160h

# --- Account connections ----------------------------------------
# Lets the account's IMAP AND SMTP hosts resolve to a private, loopback or
# link-local address — needed if your mail server genuinely lives on the
# same network as this instance. One switch covers both hosts and every
# connection to them: the check when an account is added, and every later
# sync, IDLE, writeback and send. Each of those resolves the host, refuses it
# unless every address is public, and connects to the address it checked — so
# a host that resolved public when it was added cannot later be pointed at
# this instance's own network (DNS rebinding) and handed the account's
# password. Off by default: without it, an authenticated caller could use
# account validation as a port-scan oracle against whatever the container can
# reach. Affects account connections only; the image proxy and web push keep
# the strict rule regardless.
# MAILROOM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_ACCOUNT_HOSTS=

# --- Sender authentication --------------------------------------
# Comma-separated Authentication-Results authserv-id(s) this deployment
# trusts. It gates TWO things: letting a passing DKIM/DMARC result suppress
# the "someone is claiming your own domain" warning, and letting the reading
# pane's sender-authentication notice show anything at all. Both trust ONLY
# headers whose authserv-id is in this list — an Authentication-Results
# header is otherwise attacker-writable (RFC 8601 §5 strips only a header
# bearing the relay's OWN id), so a sender could add their own `dmarc=pass`
# to cancel a genuine `dmarc=fail` if this were unset or wrong.
# Default empty, which is FAIL-SAFE but not silent-in-the-same-direction for
# both: no result is believed, so the spoofing warning fires MORE (on
# own-domain mail it might otherwise have suppressed) while the
# authentication notice goes SILENT (it cannot tell a real verdict from a
# forged one, so it shows no authentication information rather than risk a
# forged one). The value is whichever id your OWN mail provider stamps on
# mail it delivers to you — read it off the start of a real
# Authentication-Results header on an already-delivered message, before the
# first `;` (e.g. a header starting `Authentication-Results: mx.example.com;`
# means the value is `mx.example.com`); do not guess it.
# An entry may carry a received-time bound, `id<YYYY-MM-DD`: that id is trusted
# only for mail whose INTERNALDATE (when your provider delivered it, never the
# sender's Date header) is strictly before 00:00 UTC on that date. Use it for a
# provider that changed the id it stamps: RFC 8601 §5 strips only the CURRENT
# id, so after the change a forged header bearing the old id is no longer
# stripped and must not be trusted for new mail. For example:
#   MAILROOM_AUTHSERV_IDS=mx.provider.example,old.provider.example<2026-07-01
# A malformed bound is a startup error. Pick the date from
# `mailroom-admin auth-report --authserv` (the day after the old id's last
# topmost date).
# With an id configured the notice reads "unauthenticated" for any inbound mail
# without a trusted header, including mail that carries only untrusted ones (a
# header the sender can write must not turn a warning off). Locally authored mail
# and mail in Sent or Drafts shows nothing; a From of your own address does not
# count. Bounded entries never apply to mail imported from an archive.
# MAILROOM_AUTHSERV_IDS=

# --- Signed-in devices: the client address behind a reverse proxy -------
# Comma-separated IP addresses or CIDRs of the reverse proxies in front of
# Mailroom (for example 172.18.0.0/16 for the Docker network your proxy shares
# with it). Settings > Security lists each signed-in device with the address it
# last connected from; behind a proxy that address is the proxy's unless you say
# which proxies to believe. When a request comes from a listed proxy, Mailroom
# reads X-Forwarded-For right to left and shows the first address that is not
# itself a listed proxy; a request from anywhere else shows its own address and
# its header is ignored. Empty (the default) trusts none. A malformed entry is a
# startup error. DISPLAY ONLY: nothing is allowed or refused by this address.
# MAILROOM_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# --- Local DKIM check (ADR-036) -----------------------------------------
# Default ON. Mail that arrives with NO Authentication-Results header from a
# trusted id (above) has its DKIM signatures verified by Mailroom itself, before
# the message is stored, using DNS lookups (each bounded to 3 seconds, at most
# 4 lookups and 8 seconds per message). A signature that passes with a signing
# domain aligned to the From domain makes the reading pane say "Verified by
# Mailroom (DKIM, <domain>)" instead of "unauthenticated", and counts as an
# affirmative pass for the own-domain spoofing warning. A failure is never
# shown, a DNS failure stores nothing ("not checked"), and mail already stored
# is not re-checked. Needs outbound DNS. 0/false/no/off disables it entirely.
# MAILROOM_LOCAL_DKIM=

# --- Ingest-only mode (ADR-028 §2) -------------------------------------
# Instance-wide writeback kill switch: when ON, Mailroom never writes anything
# back to an IMAP server (moves, flags, expunges), regardless of any
# per-account setting — the safety catch for a cutover window that lives in
# the deployment's environment rather than a database row someone could
# un-tick. Default off (writeback follows each account's own setting).
#
# `1`, `true`, `yes`, `on` turn it on; `0`, `false`, `no`, `off` turn it off.
# Case does not matter and surrounding space is ignored. Unset and empty both
# mean off, which is what this file's commented-out form leaves.
#
# ⚠ ANY OTHER VALUE REFUSES TO BOOT, naming this variable. Until that
# fix, `enabled`, `disabled`, `ture` and `y` were each read as OFF — measured
# — so a mistyped kill switch left writeback live while the interface
# correctly reported "live" and looked like it was working properly. The same
# now applies to every MAILROOM_* boolean.
#
# After setting it, the boot log carries the WARN "writeback is DISABLED
# instance-wide". No such line AND no boot failure means the variable is not
# set at all.
# MAILROOM_WRITEBACK_DISABLED=true

# --- Send ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# How long a submission sits cancelable (Undo Send) before it actually goes
# out. Default 10s; 0 sends immediately with no undo.
# MAILROOM_UNDO_SEND_WINDOW=10s

# --- Rate limits (spec §10) ---------------------------------------------------
# The per-user API request limit (requests/second, burst). Defaults 50/s,
# burst 100 (api.DefaultRateLimitPerSecond/Burst). `0` in EITHER value
# disables the whole limiter (internal/api/ratelimit.go's `Enabled` requires
# BOTH > 0) — an explicit operator opt-out, not a typo. A negative value is
# refused outright at boot rather than silently treated as 0.
# MAILROOM_RATE_LIMIT_RPS=50
# MAILROOM_RATE_LIMIT_BURST=100
# The separate budget for the ANONYMOUS WKD key-lookup surface, which
# carries no identity to charge. Unset means the default (ON), not zero.
# MAILROOM_WKD_RATE_LIMIT_RPS=5
# MAILROOM_WKD_RATE_LIMIT_BURST=20

# --- Attachment scanning tuning (ADR-027) ------------------------------------
# Largest attachment sent to the scanner. MUST match the clamd daemon's own
# StreamMaxLength (deploy/clamd.conf) — the shipped clamav/clamav image
# measures 104857600 (100 MB), which is the default here, not the 25 MB
# upstream's own docs describe.
# MAILROOM_CLAMD_MAX_BYTES=104857600

# --- Container memory limits ------------------------------------------
# Every compose service carries a default mem_limit, sized from measurement on
# a small instance (see deploy/compose.yml and deploy/README.md). The two that
# scale with the size of the mailbox(es) synced — mailroom and postgres — are
# the ones most worth revisiting for a large corpus; measure with
# mailroom-census and size the host from that rather than
# guessing. Uncomment and change only the ones you need to.
# MAILROOM_POSTGRES_MEM_LIMIT=1g
# MAILROOM_POSTGRES_MEM_RESERVATION=256m
# MAILROOM_CRYPTO_MEM_LIMIT=384m
# MAILROOM_CLAMAV_SIGNATURES_MEM_LIMIT=2g
# MAILROOM_CLAMD_MEM_LIMIT=3500m
# MAILROOM_SERVER_MEM_LIMIT=1g
